{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai</b> — What is the relevance of the sabbatical year (shemittah) to Mount Sinai? Were not all the commandments stated from Sinai? Rather, just as shemittah — its general principles, its specific details, and its precise rulings were stated from Sinai, so too all of them — their general principles, their specific details, and their precise rulings were stated from Sinai. Thus it is taught in Torat Kohanim (at the beginning of the parashah). Now, it appears to me that this is its meaning: since we do not find that the sabbatical of the land (shemittat karka'ot) was repeated in the plains of Moab, we would have learned that its general principles and its specific details were all stated from Sinai — and the verse comes and teaches concerning every divine address (dibbur) that was spoken to Moses at Sinai, that from Sinai were their general principles and their precise rulings, and they went back and were repeated in the plains of Moab. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 25:1). But this is not correct in my eyes at all, for there are many commandments like shemittah that were not repeated in the plains of Moab, and yet we know concerning them that their general principles and their specific details were stated from Sinai or at the Tent of Meeting. And furthermore, from where do we know that the other matters that were repeated in the plains of Moab were juxtaposed to shemittah, and that their general principles were not from Sinai while their specific details were from the plains of Moab? And it would have been more fitting to say that those repeated were repeated in order to explain their specifics, for at Sinai only their general principles were stated. Rather, the beraita taught in Torat Kohanim has a clear meaning: that shemittah — its general principles were stated from Sinai, as it is written in the parashah of \"And these are the judgments\" — \"but the seventh year you shall let it rest and lie fallow, that the poor of your people may eat\" (Exodus 23:11), and these are the laws of shemittah in general terms; and here He returned and said that on Mount Sinai all its specifics were additionally stated, for indeed they are all mentioned in this parashah. And at the end of the matter (Leviticus 27:34) it is written: \"These are the commandments which YHWH commanded Moses for the children of Israel on Mount Sinai\" — to juxtapose all the commandments to the shemittah mentioned, that all of them were likewise stated in general principle and in specific detail, and all from Sinai. And thus they taught there in Torat Kohanim (end of Sifra): \"These are the commandments\" — no prophet is permitted to innovate anything further from now on. \"Which YHWH commanded Moses\" — the agent is worthy of the One who sent him. \"Moses ... to the children of Israel\" — the merit of Israel brought it about. \"On Mount Sinai\" — that all of them were stated from Sinai. Thus far Torat Kohanim. But the matter of the plains of Moab is that Moses was commanded there to expound the Torah to the coming generations. So it appears to me, and this is the central principle and its flowering.\n\nAs for the substance of this parashah written here, R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Leviticus 25:1) that this is the covenant written in the parashah of \"And these are the judgments\" (Exodus 24:7), and when they accepted upon themselves \"we will do and we will hear,\" then the covenant was struck and He declared to them this entire parashah. And He mentioned it in this place in order to join the conditions of the Land, for just as He said concerning forbidden relations that on their account the Land would vomit them out (Leviticus 18:28), so too He said in the parashah of \"If in My statutes\" concerning the sabbaths of the Land (Leviticus 26:34) — and it was necessary to mention the sabbaths beforehand.\n\nIn my opinion, it was written here in proper sequence, for the meaning of \"on Mount Sinai\" is: when he went up there to receive the second tablets. And the explanation of the matter is: at the beginning of the first forty days of the first tablets, Moses wrote in the Book of the Covenant all the words of YHWH and all the judgments stated there, and he sprinkled the blood of the covenant on the people (Exodus 24:8). And when they sinned with the calf and the tablets were broken, it was as though that covenant was nullified before the Holy One, blessed be He. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled to Moses with the second tablets, He commanded him in a new covenant, as it is written: \"Behold, I make a covenant\" (Exodus 34:10). And He restored there the weighty commandments that had been stated in the parashah of \"And these are the judgments\" under the first covenant, and He said: \"Write for yourself these words, for according to the mouth of these words I have made a covenant with you and with Israel\" (Exodus 34:27). And the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to make stringent upon them in this second covenant that it would be upon them with imprecations and with curses, and that it would be like the first — upon all the first commandments and upon all the judgments, as it is stated concerning the first covenant: \"all the words of YHWH and all the judgments\" (Exodus 24:3). Therefore He said here at the end of the reproofs (Leviticus 26:46): \"These are the statutes and the judgments and the teachings which YHWH gave between Himself and the children of Israel on Mount Sinai by the hand of Moses\" — this alludes to all the commandments and the judgments that were stated in the first covenant in the parashah of \"And these are the judgments,\" that they were all included in this covenant. Now, in the first Book of the Covenant the sabbatical year was mentioned in general, as I noted, for it is written \"but the seventh year you shall let it rest and lie fallow\" etc. (Exodus 23:11), and now in this second covenant it was stated with its specifics, its precise rulings, and its penalties.\n\nAnd at the time of the first covenant — which was during the first forty days — Moses was commanded concerning the Tabernacle. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, was reconciled to him and commanded him to strike for them a second covenant, Moses went down \"and commanded them all that YHWH had commanded him on Mount Sinai\" (Exodus 34:34), and the making of the Tabernacle was included among them. And then Moses assembled all the congregation of the children of Israel and said to them, as at the beginning: \"These are the words which YHWH has commanded to do them\" (Exodus 35:1) — concerning the work of the Tabernacle. Now, they accepted the matter with joy, and they went out from before him immediately, and they all came and gave the donation and made the Tabernacle and completed its work. And when it was erected, immediately \"He called to Moses, and YHWH spoke to him from the Tent of Meeting\" (Leviticus 1:1), and He commanded him concerning the offerings and all the priestly Torah, and Moses commanded everything to Aaron and to his sons and to all the children of Israel immediately. And when he had completed, he said to them: HaShem further commanded me on Mount Sinai to explain to you the shemittah and the Jubilee, and to strike with you concerning all the commandment and the judgments a new covenant with imprecation and oath. And there was no need now that he should slaughter sacrifices and sprinkle half the blood on the people and half the blood on the altar as he did with the first, but they accepted the first covenant upon themselves with these imprecations and curses. And this is the meaning of \"which YHWH gave between Himself and the children of Israel on Mount Sinai\" (Leviticus 26:46) — for He is the One who gives them this covenant, for He forgave them on the basis of these conditions, as I explained at the end of Ki Tissa (Exodus 34:27). And likewise the covenant of the plains of Moab was so, that they accepted the Torah upon themselves with those imprecations and curses, and it is the covenant, as it is written: \"These are the words of the covenant which YHWH commanded Moses to strike with the children of Israel in the land of Moab, besides the covenant which He struck with them at Horeb\" (Deuteronomy 28:69). And they spoke truth in saying that the conditions of the Land are shemittah and Jubilee, as He mentions in the reproofs (below 26:34–35), and it was on this basis that they were renewed in this second covenant."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase וזה כפתור ופרח (literally 'and this is its capital and flower,' alluding to the ornamentation of the Tabernacle's pillars) is an idiom meaning 'this is the central principle and its flowering/detail'; rendered literally per the cryptic-style rail but the idiom is well-attested in rabbinic Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:53.455835855Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}